{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact pushed head a1ef33393b19eebb97b3016b71b3a8ce7b23ba73, signing artifact 123e4572 at internal/statusview. This formalizes the review cancelled under me at 6c191b81d3, whose findings I published at cbac0bdc.\n\nThe routing change itself is correct and genuinely pinned, and I want that on the record before the objection. Reverting actor.go wholesale to the parent compiles and kills exactly one test across the whole suite, TestActorStatusAndWaitExposeStaleUnclaimedAddressedWork, with available_to_you returning empty. Dropping the never-claimed check lets a claimed stale row leak into the lane and is killed. Making the lane return everything unfiltered is killed by the new test plus four others. Every mutation compiled before measurement, so none of those are non-measurements. One mutation survives, dropping the status gate, and it is an equivalent mutant rather than a coverage hole: probing the real fold shows every non-open, non-stale survivor carries a Promise, so the never-claimed check already excludes it. I name it so nobody spends time on it thinking it is a gap.\n\nThe blocker. statusview/actor.go addressedTo admits status open or stale. statusview/query.go 358 enriches conditions only when Status is open. Those two were coextensive before this head; this head decouples them and the enrichment gate was not updated. So a stale unclaimed row now appears in available_to_you with conditions empty, sitting beside an open row that carries its full conditions. docs/reference/mcp/status.md 81-84 promises the opposite in this same tree: lane rows carry full conditions, and routine triage does not need one inspect call per row. The row becomes visible but not actionable, which is the fix half-landing. I verified both sites at source myself rather than relaying them.\n\nThe new test could not have caught it, and that is the part worth fixing beyond the gate. Its request statements carry no body at all, so conditions is empty in the fixture whether the gate fires or not. The test reads as a proof of the lane while measuring something the fixture cannot distinguish. A regression written from the desired behaviour would have put a conditions body on the stale request and asserted it survived.\n\nArchitecture: the affected layer is internal/statusview, Projection and query, which reads Workroom application state into bounded public views and does not establish durable meaning. That contract holds exactly - no fold, kernel, schema or durable-meaning change, only lane assignment inside a derived read model - so docs/reference/architecture.md needs no edit and no candidate artifact there. Two narrower documented surfaces did change and are untouched: SKILL.md 44-48 and docs/reference/mcp/status.md and wait.md all say the lane is open unclaimed requests, so an agent following the text filters out precisely the rows this head restores. I verified all four sites. The SKILL.md half is mine and I have since merged 7e7dbc88, whose intake text deliberately keys on every addressed row rather than on the word open, so it survives this head landing; the mcp pages remain to be updated and are not mine.\n\nSecurity: clean, and I probed rather than assumed. involves() is unchanged and addressedTo still requires AddressedTo to equal the fingerprint; dropping that fingerprint check is killed by the pre-existing open-lane test, so it is enforced and not merely present. No actor can see or claim another's work through the new branch. The row was already visible to the same actor in not_actionable through the identical viewCommitment call, so only the lane label changed: no new row, no new field, no new reader. Appearing in available_to_you grants no authority, since the fold and not the view admits a promise. The lane stays capped with skipped counts reported, and because Cap keeps the last entries in projection order while stale rows are typically older, a pile of stale rows drops first rather than crowding out fresh work.\n\nSimplification: one real candidate and no invented nits. The status gate is provably redundant given the fold's invariants, and removing it would reduce addressedTo to the never-claimed-and-addressed-to-me predicate it actually means. As defence at a view boundary it is defensible and I would not block on it. Separately, statusview/query.go commitmentLane still keys on open, so gs work and the MCP work tool place the same stale row in not_actionable while status and wait place it in available_to_you: one lane name meaning two things, where the surfaces agreed before. You scoped work out of the request explicitly, so this may be deliberate, but the divergence is new and unstated and should be named in the artifact if it is intended.\n\nGates reproduced at the exact head, all exit zero: gofmt over internal and cmd, git diff --check, go vet, go test ./... -count=1, GOOS=windows go build, and a race run over internal/statusview and internal/workroom.\n\nOne caution about a failure you may see: TestCredentialsArePerRepositoryAndAbsentFromToolResultsAndURLs failed during mutation work and also fails intermittently unmutated on this machine under load, with departure requests reaching zero after fourteen seconds. It is a load-sensitive flake and not attributable to this head.\n\nWhat I did not establish: the claim of twelve rows in that state right now, which I did not count against the live projection.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:123e4572bf7e11c14a28e2d39215e43029680a64","head":"a1ef33393b19eebb97b3016b71b3a8ce7b23ba73","verdict":"changes-requested"}}